 \begin{abstract}
-A proof that a hidden coordinate satisfies a task policy does not establish that uncertain physical evidence supports that claim. We study report compliance when authenticated sources issue private spacetime uncertainty boxes and some sources may be Byzantine. Acceptance requires every threshold-supported state to satisfy the task policy. Two reachability predicates distinguish validity, invalidity, boundary ambiguity, and inconsistent evidence. We implement exact lower-corner and subset-enumeration circuits with signature verification in Groth16. The depth circuit has a polynomial bound for fixed dimension, but subset enumeration is cheaper in every tested small-fault-budget case. Across 27 synthetic conditions with 30 randomized repetitions, errors appear when calibration or fault assumptions are violated. Under the stated assumptions, mean decisive coverage is 87.52\%, compared with 75.63\% for a fixed-margin rule, with no observed classification errors. A replay of 787 UrbanNav reference states with simulated evidence yields 93.27\% decisive coverage versus 81.94\%. On an Apple M1 workstation, the subset circuits produce 164-byte proofs with median proving times of 163--255~ms; the depth circuits require 213--1091~ms. These results establish prototype feasibility while identifying calibration and deployment conditions that cryptographic verification alone cannot establish.
