 \section{Limitations and deployment implications}
-The prototype answers a narrower question than complete vehicular report authenticity. A sound spatial certificate does not establish the truth of sensed content, prevent relay attacks, prove physical possession of a sensor, or validate a claimed calibration profile. Correct enrollment, event binding, and trustworthy uncertainty issuance are indispensable external conditions.
+The certificate concerns the report's reference event, not the truth of its sensed content. It also leaves relay attacks, physical sensor possession, and validation of claimed calibration profiles unresolved. Acceptance depends on correct enrollment, event binding, and trustworthy issuance of uncertainty boxes outside the circuit.
 
 Full-roster signatures avoid silent omission within an accepted witness, but a malicious source can block completion. A retry must use an authorized new roster and matching fault policy; allowing the vehicle to drop inconvenient evidence without such authorization changes the security model. Distinct-key checks alone do not prevent Sybil authorities. Public rosters can also reveal where a vehicle obtained evidence.
 
-Backend selection depends on the omission budget and dimension. The measured cases favor subset enumeration; the depth construction supplies a threshold-flexible alternative, with no measured crossover yet. Comparisons with complete private-fusion systems require aligned leakage, liveness, geometry, adversaries, and hardware.
