 \section{Conclusion}
-This work connects robust evidence fusion to a report-bound, publicly verifiable policy certificate. Its exact four-valued relation distinguishes supported decisions from ambiguity and inconsistent evidence while keeping signed boxes private. Two implemented circuits realize the same semantics, with subset enumeration offering lower costs throughout the measured small omission regime. The experiments characterize the tradeoff between decision availability and uncertainty tolerance, and show how source assumptions affect correctness. These results establish a concrete proof interface for robust spacetime compliance and motivate subsequent validation with calibrated multisource evidence and integrated vehicular testbeds.
+Robust spacetime compliance asks whether all threshold-supported states meet a policy, rather than whether one selected coordinate does. The report-bound certificate makes this decision publicly verifiable while keeping signed boxes private, and separates valid and invalid outcomes from ambiguity and inconsistent evidence.
+
+Both implemented circuits evaluate that exact relation. Subset enumeration costs less throughout the measured small omission regime; increasing uncertainty tolerance changes decision availability, while failures of the source assumptions can produce incorrect decisions. The next deployment question is whether calibrated multisource evidence can be supplied reliably in an integrated vehicular testbed.
 
